WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.620
Hettle windows and flowers transmit more ultraviolet and infrared radiation, warming interiors, and reducing microbial abundance. This is Planetterrian Daily, Episode 206. Some flowers have small, semi-translucent sections in their petals. Field experiments showed these windows transmitted more ultraviolet and infrared radiation than pigmented tissue. Internal floral temperature predicted microbial numbers, with warmer flowers harboring fewer microbes.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:29.840
microbes.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.120
Microbial communities inside flowers can affect nectar quality.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.380
Corresponding author Jessica Williams noted that a small floral trait can change the

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.380
physical environment inside a flower.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:46.640
Most people assume flower color exists only to attract pollinators from a distance.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:52.580
In reality, the semi-translucent windows also change the physical environment inside the

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:53.220
bloom itself.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.660
Light and heat that pass through those windows raise internal temperature enough to limit

00:00:58.660 --> 00:00:59.860
microbial growth.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.040
Warmer flowers in the field experiments carried

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.540
measurably fewer microbes than cooler ones.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.140
The temperature shift occurs because the

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.940
windows transmit more infrared and ultraviolet

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.300
radiation than surrounding pigmented tissue.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.500
That change in turn affects nectar quality

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.140
and the microbes that live in it.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.360
One concrete number from the work is the consistent

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.520
prediction that internal temperature alone

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.900
explained microbial abundance across the tested species.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.600
A new study calculated the reef's potential gross ecosystem product.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.540
The figure represents trillions of dollars in goods and services delivered each year.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.220
Benefits span human well-being and planetary health.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.980
Full potential requires restoration and protection of oyster reefs worldwide.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.780
The work quantifies ecological contributions that parallel gross economic product measures.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:57.500
The Oyster Reef Study assigns a gross ecosystem product value of $12 trillion annually to

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:58.180
these habitats.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.720
That figure captures services ranging from water filtration to coastal protection and

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:04.640
fisheries support.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.100
Restoration would be required to realize the full amount because many reefs have already

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.540
been lost or degraded.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:17.000
The calculation treats ecological output the way economists treat gross domestic product,

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.760
giving planners a single comparable metric.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:26.340
Dr. Jamaladin Jampa conducts research on degradation of coastal waters in South Sulawesi.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.760
The studies examine how breaking ecological relationships in marine ecosystems can

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:32.340
be restored.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:37.640
Local conservation efforts connect directly to broader climate resilience outcomes.

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:43.060
Restoration of those relationships supports stability across larger ocean systems.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:49.240
Work in Indonesia demonstrates ties between regional actions and planetary environmental goals.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:54.080
Dr. Jampa's projects emphasize practical recovery of marine habitats.

